WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:07.329
Today is September 30, 2026.

3
00:00:07.329 --> 00:00:15.356
Overnight, Wall Street absorbed a tense standoff between surging long-dated Treasury yields and stabilizing global energy flows.

4
00:00:15.356 --> 00:00:22.574
The S and P five hundred slipped slightly by seventeen basis points, hovering around seventy-six hundred and twenty.

5
00:00:22.574 --> 00:00:30.160
The Nasdaq eased nine basis points, continuing a quarter-long consolidation, while the Dow closed modestly lower.

6
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.983
The primary macro catalyst remains the bond market.

7
00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:41.140
The thirty-year Treasury yield climbed for a sixth consecutive session to reach levels unseen since 2002.

8
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:51.336
This bond sell-off is not driven by energy inflation, as West Texas Intermediate crude fell sharply following restored Middle Eastern pipeline flows and diplomatic talks.

9
00:00:51.336 --> 00:01:00.589
Instead, markets are aggressively repricing a higher-for-longer neutral rate driven by resilient economic expansion and the unwinding of the yen carry trade.

10
00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:04.792
Yet, monetary expectations show a sharp divergence.

11
00:01:04.792 --> 00:01:10.429
Fed funds futures price a greater than fifty percent probability of an October rate hike.

12
00:01:10.429 --> 00:01:18.505
In contrast, New York Fed President John Williams indicated no immediate urgency to hike, suggesting a patient pause.

13
00:01:18.505 --> 00:01:32.599
Wall Street consensus historically notes the Federal Reserve rarely hikes just weeks before a November midterm election, suggesting current terminal rate fears may be overextended ahead of incoming PCE inflation and Friday non-farm payroll prints.

14
00:01:32.599 --> 00:01:41.557
Turning to technology and enterprise capital flows, relentless artificial intelligence spending continues to buffer broader macro liquidity constraints.

15
00:01:41.557 --> 00:01:47.415
The White House recently reaffirmed zero regulatory pauses on frontier AI development.

16
00:01:47.415 --> 00:01:51.765
Corporate balance sheets validate this historic infrastructure ramp.

17
00:01:51.765 --> 00:02:01.594
Anthropic is pacing toward a hundred-billion-dollar annualized run rate, even while detailing extensive autonomous alignment risks in its massive IPO prospectus.

18
00:02:01.594 --> 00:02:10.527
Meanwhile, OpenAI annualized revenue approaches seventy billion dollars, expanding its enterprise footprint with autonomous agent platforms.

19
00:02:10.527 --> 00:02:27.782
Upstream, AMD announced an eight point two billion dollar all-stock acquisition of World Labs to bolster physical world modeling against Nvidia, while SpaceX achieved full orbital capability with its five-thousand-ton Starship, unlocking early orbital data center compute contracts with hyperscalers.

20
00:02:27.782 --> 00:02:31.777
The structural capex cycle remains fiercely intact.

21
00:02:31.777 --> 00:02:38.273
For individual equities on our institutional radar today, three distinct setups dominate order flow.

22
00:02:38.273 --> 00:02:44.596
First, Micron Technology reports earnings today in a critical test for hardware fundamentals.

23
00:02:44.596 --> 00:02:52.243
Consensus estimates look for earnings per share of thirty-one dollars and sixteen cents on revenue surpassing fifty billion dollars.

24
00:02:52.243 --> 00:03:00.429
Elevated expectations hinge entirely on guidance for next-generation high-bandwidth memory capacity and enterprise storage demand.

25
00:03:00.429 --> 00:03:07.231
Investors are watching the critical two-thousand-dollar valuation benchmark as a barometer for the broader compute trade.

26
00:03:07.231 --> 00:03:10.834
Second, Tesla faces a pivotal crossroad.

27
00:03:10.834 --> 00:03:20.037
Morgan Stanley reiterated a four-hundred-dollar price target, modeling long-term monetization around robotaxi software and autonomous subscriptions.

28
00:03:20.037 --> 00:03:22.795
Yet operational headwinds linger.

29
00:03:22.795 --> 00:03:31.912
Second-generation Roadster unveilings have slipped to mid-October, and third-quarter vehicle delivery numbers due Friday risk showing year-over-year contraction.

30
00:03:31.912 --> 00:03:38.885
Near-term price support rests near the two-hundred-day moving average, with resistance capped below summer highs.

31
00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:44.228
Finally, Fair Isaac Corporation suffered an aggressive institutional liquidation.

32
00:03:44.228 --> 00:03:54.400
The Federal Housing Finance Agency announced structural mortgage pricing reforms, integrating VantageScore alongside legacy FICO scores across Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac.

33
00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:01.079
This dismantles a multi-year administrative monopoly, triggering an immediate compression in forward multiples.

34
00:04:01.079 --> 00:04:08.481
The stock has broken below its multi-year channel support, with technical risk skewed toward the four-hundred-and-fifty-dollar zone.

35
00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:12.488
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

36
00:04:12.488 --> 00:04:16.655
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
